Patch 2048: J-Stars' shadow maps compared as the game reads them (ZPASS_ARGB_TXP=0, RSX_POLY_OFFSET=0, RSX_FP_INLINE_K=0 off).

J-Stars reads its two shadow maps (D24S8 surfaces, t7 2048x1024 and t8 1024x1024) through an A8R8G8B8 view with a
depth func in TEXTURE_ADDRESS. Its materials take TXP taps and sum .x as comparison results; its fog pass reads the
same kind of view with TEX and rebuilds depth from the bytes. Only DEPTH24/DEPTH16 views were compared here, so every
material read "lit": the floor of Yoshiwara under the roof came out washed out (a SHADOW_NULL test darkened it).
After the v0.15 port (ZPASS_ARGB_TXP, polygon offset on casters):
- such a view read with TXP becomes a shadow comparison (patch 2017's helper), judged as the DEPTH24_D8 view it is;
  it samples the byte copy of the surface unremapped and rebuilds the 24-bit depth from its A/R/G bytes (_rsx_d24);
- the guest polygon offset (SET_POLY_OFFSET_FILL_ENABLE, SCALE_FACTOR, BIAS: new rsx_state fields) becomes the D3D
  depth bias, through bits 48..55 of the depth key;
- the comparison decompile path emits "buffered" constants (a b1 cbuffer of fp_constants[] / fp_alpha) that do not
  match this backend's b1 slot (rsx_texscale / rsx_alphatest / fp_k): every program with a compared unit read garbage
  constants and drew black. They are put back inline, as the plain decompiler emits them.
Diagnostic: SHADOW_BIAS=<f> is subtracted from the comparison reference.

Tested (US disc, bench.ps1 exp line): demo 20.83 and Free Battle 21.58 flips/s (bench\exp\2026-11-07_1442-inlinek);
the Yoshiwara floor now has the roof's and the statue's shadows and darker tiles, as in the v0.15 Training picture;
characters lit and shaded normally in the demo and the fight.

